{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789405848,"nonce":"4ASooO+0l4Os579IkzyMOrE2MCE=","title":"Python: an in-language string builder and str(int)/str(float) in HIR","message":"Text is built as `parts.append(f\"{k}={v}\")` then `\",\".join(parts)`, or\n`s += piece` in a loop. Each f-string piece is a plugin call and a\nfresh allocation, `str(i)` goes through `$String$from_int`, and `+`\ncopies both sides. bench_tokenize.py builds 300,000 tokens this way\nand pays about 0.2 s of its 1.6 s there.\n\nFix: a length-counted, geometrically grown builder in the built-in\nlibrary that formatting lowers into directly (append bytes, append int,\nappend float with the repr rules), so an f-string or a `join` is one\nallocation; and integer and float formatting as HIR rather than FFI so\nthey inline into the loop. The `+=` accumulator already lowers to a\nbuilder shape; the same builder should back it.","files":null}]}